  "articlePanels": [
    {
      "kind": "lede",
      "html": "<p>A care group with homes in Carlisle and Dumfries works in two countries. Each home has a different regulator and different standards. Wales, Northern Ireland and Ireland differ again. If you audit care homes, you meet all of this.</p>"
    },
    {
      "kind": "takeaways",
      "heading": "The short version",
      "html": "<ul><li>Five regulators: CQC in England, the Care Inspectorate in Scotland, Care Inspectorate Wales, RQIA in Northern Ireland, and HIQA in the Republic of Ireland.</li><li>Each has its own rules, grades and returns. Use the ones for the home you are in.</li><li>All five ask much the same thing. Is the home safe, well led, and honest about its own quality?</li><li>Your work on site is nearly the same in all five.</li><li>You need one way of working that can carry five sets of rules.</li></ul>"
    },
    {
      "kind": "default",
      "eyebrow": "The five",
      "heading": "Who regulates what",
      "html": "<p>The English regulator is not the British one.</p><p><strong>England:</strong> the Care Quality Commission.</p><p><strong>Scotland:</strong> the Care Inspectorate. It grades homes on a six-point scale.</p><p><strong>Wales:</strong> Care Inspectorate Wales.</p><p><strong>Northern Ireland:</strong> the Regulation and Quality Improvement Authority, RQIA.</p><p><strong>Republic of Ireland:</strong> the Health Information and Quality Authority, HIQA.</p><p>Each is its own regime. Use the current rules for the nation the home is in.</p>"
    },
    {
      "kind": "default",
      "eyebrow": "What is the same",
      "heading": "What all five ask",
      "html": "<p>Is the home safe? Is it well led? Are people treated with dignity? Does the home look at its own quality honestly, and act?</p><p>The names and grades differ. The questions do not. If your audit finds the facts and records the evidence, you answer all five.</p>"
    },
    {
      "kind": "default",
      "eyebrow": "The problem",
      "heading": "Five rule sets, and the pull of five systems",
      "html": "<p>Groups in more than one nation often end up with one way of working in each. The spreadsheets do not match. Head office cannot compare homes.</p><p>The rules must differ. The method need not.</p>"
    },
    {
      "kind": "default",
      "eyebrow": "One method",
      "heading": "Where Quickler fits",
      "html": "<p>Quickler does not hold any regulator's standards. You build your own Check. You use it the same way in each nation. You answer in WhatsApp by text, voice note or photo. Quickler writes the report from what you sent.</p><p>Quickler is not linked to or approved by any regulator. It does not certify compliance. It is not a care records system. Whether your evidence is enough is for you and the inspector to judge.</p>"
    },
    {
      "kind": "faq",
      "heading": "Questions, answered",
      "html": "<h3>Who regulates care in each part of the British Isles?</h3><p>England: CQC. Scotland: the Care Inspectorate. Wales: Care Inspectorate Wales. Northern Ireland: RQIA. Republic of Ireland: HIQA. Each has its own rules.</p><h3>Is CQC the regulator for the whole UK?</h3><p>No. CQC covers England only.</p><h3>If the rules differ, can one system serve them all?</h3><p>Yes, if you build the Check yourself. Quickler holds no regulator's standards. You shape each Check to the rules that apply and answer in WhatsApp.</p><h3>Does Quickler guarantee compliance?</h3><p>No. It is not linked to any regulator and does not certify anything. It is not a care records system.</p><h3>Related reading</h3><ul><li><a href=\"/pages/what-an-inspection-is-really-testing.html\">What an inspection is really testing</a></li><li><a href=\"/pages/care-inspectorate-compliance-software-scotland.html\">Care Inspectorate compliance software (Scotland)</a></li><li><a href=\"/pages/cqc-compliance-audit-checklist-uk.html\">CQC compliance audit checklist (England)</a></li></ul>"
    }
